<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	App Store edition. The Mac App Store mandates the App Sandbox, so this build
	is sandboxed and the auto-paste flow is compiled out (APPSTORE flag): no
	Accessibility, no keystroke synthesis, no foreign-app activation. History
	items are placed on the clipboard for a manual ⌘V.

	The Developer ID / Homebrew edition uses "Clip Board.entitlements" instead —
	unsandboxed, with auto-paste. Both editions ship from the same Apple Developer
	account (team) but are signed with different certificate types: this one with
	Apple Distribution (Apple re-signs on ingestion), the other with Developer ID
	Application.

	Privacy posture: no network, no device access, no personal-information access.
	Under the App Sandbox a capability is DENIED unless its entitlement is present
	and set to true — so "we don't use X" is expressed by OMITTING the key, never
	by setting it to <false/>. Apple's sandbox validator REJECTS any sandbox
	entitlement whose value is false (Guideline 2.4.5(i) / Technical Q&A QA1773),
	which is why this file lists only the sandbox switch itself. Do NOT add
	network.client, network.server, device.audio-input, device.camera,
	personal-information.{addressbook,calendars,location}, or any other capability
	key here unless the app genuinely needs it — and if it does, set it to <true/>.

	History is AES-GCM-256 encrypted at rest with a Keychain-bound key; the sandbox
	stores it under the app's container rather than ~/Library/Application Support.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
